extraction_notes: "THE THREE AREAS OF EXPERTISE ARE VARIANTS, which is D1''s sub-type slot. Weapons overrides `sdc_base` to 30; Electrical and Mechanical take the parent''s 35. `skills_additional` UNIONS with the parent''s list rather than replacing it, which is what makes a variant the right shape here - a Hardware character is one class played three ways, not three classes. THE SECOND AREA OF EXPERTISE IS NOT MODELLED as a second variant: `applyVariant` takes one, and the trade costs three attacks per melee, which nothing here can deduct. It is stated as an ability and taken at the table. THE CONDITIONAL SKILLS ARE GRANTED UNCONDITIONALLY and say so in their notes: printed 75 and 76 give Basic Electronics or Basic Mechanics only where the character did not take the equivalent scholastically, and a class cannot see what its OCCUPATION half chose. Granting and stating is the honest direction - the alternative is withholding a skill the book gives. THE W.P. SPLIT IS A NAMED LIST, per survey D8: the catalog holds a single `Weapon Proficiencies` category of 38 rows, and Heroes Unlimited''s division is a property of the PROGRAM rather than of the weapon. The lists are NOT this repo''s invention - printed 29 enumerates them, seven Ancient and six Modern, with ENERGY WEAPONS as a THIRD group of two that neither program reaches. The first draft of this class guessed all thirteen names and got three right; four more are rows the catalog spells differently (`W.P. Target` is `W.P. Targeting`, `W.P. Rifle` is `W.P. Rifles`, `W.P. Sub-Machinegun` is `W.P. Submachine-Gun`, `W.P. Semi & Fully Automatic Rifle` is `W.P. Automatic and Semi-automatic Rifles`) and `W.P. Heavy` is `W.P. Heavy Military Weapons`. `W.P. Statf` on printed 29 is an OCR fault for Staff. `base` ON EVERY ENTRY IS THE CATALOG BASE PLUS THE PRINTED BONUS, already added, which is the frontmatter rule: Electrical Engineer is 30 in the catalog and +30% here, so 60. THE EQUIPMENT BUDGET IS NOT IMPORTED. All three areas roll on a budget table and spend it - the Mechanical character on a super vehicle built from printed 80-87, the Weapons character on gimmick weapons from printed 78-79 - and the catalog has no builder and no shape for a built vehicle. Survey D7, BOOK-INGEST-AUDIT F3."
---

## Lore

The character who falls into this category possesses no unusual super ability,
no magic power and no bionics. He or she is a normal person with an exceptional
knack for building and using machines and gimmicks. This is the one with a
suped-up car and a jet pack in the trunk.

As a crime fighter the character is likely to be physically capable, with combat
and physical skills, but the real emphasis is the machines.

The hardware character is an expert with his own devices, and many of them are
experimental, potentially dangerous, or require special training - not to
mention costly. He is not likely to outfit his super buddies with his gizmos,
for personal and for safety reasons.

**Electrical** is the whiz kid with wiring, circuitry, micro chips, computers,
listening devices and electronic gizmos of all kinds, able to use, construct,
design and modify them. **Mechanical** is the whiz kid of vehicles and
mechanical devices, the wild-eyed super inventor of suped-up cars.
**Weapons** is an authority not only in the use of weapons but in their
construction, design and modification.

## GM Notes

**The minimum I.Q. for this character is 9.** If the rolled attribute is 8 or
less, raise it to 9; if it is higher, leave it alone.

**Hand to hand combat is not automatic** and must be selected as a learned
skill. Every hero gets two attacks per melee, and more come from hand to hand
and boxing.

**The equipment budget** is where each area of expertise actually spends its
time, and none of it is in this catalog:

- **Electrical**, printed 74, rolls a budget and buys from the equipment
  section.
- **Mechanical**, printed 75, rolls a budget and BUILDS a super vehicle or two
  from printed 80-87. At least 90% of the money must go into the
  vehicle; 5% may go on other equipment and weapons, and up to 10% may be saved
  for future work and repairs. Construction needs a work facility at least the
  size of a two-car garage.
- **Weapons**, printed 78-79, buys, modifies or makes its own, and may also use
  items from the Ninjas and Superspies RPG. Printed 78 lists gimmick weapons the
  character can build or buy illegally.

Unless the character is extremely wealthy, only conventional weaponry and body
armour are available - except to the Weapons expert, who has only what he
bought, modified or made. Any alignment may be chosen, though heroes should
generally be of good alignment.
', 'published', 'data-script'
WHERE NOT EXISTS (SELECT 1 FROM imported_classes WHERE class_id = 'hu-hardware');


-- Read the result back rather than trusting the exit code. d1-apply prints
-- these, and a CR in the stored markdown means the checkout mangled the file.
SELECT class_id, name, status, length(markdown) AS bytes, instr(markdown, char(13)) > 0 AS has_cr
  FROM imported_classes WHERE class_id = 'hu-hardware';

